# Language Detection and Remediation Matching

Ensures remediation code examples match the target application programming language.

## Language Detection

At the start of security analysis, identify the primary language:
- Analyze file extensions and count (.py, .js, .java, .go, .rs, .rb, etc.)
- Detect framework when possible (FastAPI, Django, Spring Boot, Express, Next.js, Rails, etc.)
- Store as TARGET_LANGUAGE and TARGET_FRAMEWORK context

## Language-Aware Remediation

### If Remediation Language Matches Target Language
- Use the code examples as-is
- Ensure proper syntax highlighting

### If Remediation Language Does NOT Match Target Language
Provide a language compatibility notice explaining the security pattern conceptually and listing implementation notes specific to the target language with recommended libraries.

### If No Specific Guidance Exists
- Provide generic remediation in the target language
- Clearly mark as Generic Remediation

## Core Principle

Never show code examples in the wrong language. Always provide language-appropriate guidance or clear notices when exact matches are not available.
